-- title:
--   The finite code space of the compression
-- statement:
--   These notions belong to a counterexample to the duality conjecture for metric entropy. For every $a,b\ge1$ there are $n$ and a symmetric convex body $K\subset\mathbb R^n$ with $\log N(K,Q_n)>b\log N(Q_n^\circ,a^{-1}K^\circ)$. Here $Q_n=[-1,1]^n$ is the cube, $K^\circ=\{y:\langle x,y\rangle\le1\text{ for all }x\in K\}$ is the polar (so $Q_n^\circ$ is the $\ell_1$ ball), and $N(A,B)$ is the least number of translates of $B$ covering $A$. A nonnegative column weighting $\mu$ with $\sum_y\mu_y\le1$ on columns $y=(v_y,T_y)$ gives the profile mixture $x\mapsto\sum_y\mu_y\varphi_h(d_{T_y}(x,v_y))$; compression approximates all such mixtures uniformly by a list whose size does not depend on the number of rows.
--
--   1. **Codes.** For parameters $u,q,h,s,N$, a compression code consists of a common index $i\in\{1..u\}$, a label code in $\{1..q\}$ for each level $j<h$ and pivot slot $k<s$, and a weight code in $\{1..N\}$ for each level, slot and subset $T\subseteq\{1..u\}$. So there are
--   $$
--   u\cdot q^{hs}\cdot N^{hs2^u}
--   $$
--   codes.
--   2. **Image.** For a decoder from codes to functions, the decoded family is the finite image of the code space.
